A||||5||mrfixit58||How to ID a 5-speed Getrag???||||5||
Z||000000||mrfixit58||05-06-2000||04:43 PM||||I just bought a bunch of parts and a couple of parts cars. In a bunch of loose parts was a manual tranny from an 87 GT. The guy said that when he took the car apart the shift plate had the 5-speed shift pattern but he really wasn't sure if it was a 5-speed or a 4-speed. Is there an easy way to tell them apart? Is there a part number on the case that will help me confirm it's identy? Any tips???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>Thanks,<BR>Roy||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||05-06-2000||08:26 PM||||If he took the transmission from an 87 it is a five speed.  The four speed transmissions stopped at 86 1/2 Fiero V-6.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||05-06-2000||08:30 PM||||Quickest way to tell is to see where the shift cables hook up. All other Fiero transaxles have the 2 cables side-by-side, but the Getrag has one on the top and the other down farther and towards the front of the car. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||05-06-2000||09:47 PM||||There are a couple of ways to determine if the tranny is a 5-sp. Getrag or not.<P>One way is the end plate (opposite from the bellhousing).  On the Getrag the plate is bolted to the tranny.....other years it was part of the case.<P>Another way to tell is the Getrags had a separate dip stick.  The '86-'87 was  silver and unscrewed the '88's were black or red that flipped up and off.<P>Yet another way to tell is by looking inside the case (where the inpit shaft is.  You should see two circular covers on the Getrag.<P>Then of course there is the cable brackets, like what SCCA Fiero suggested.<P>I hope this helps.<P><BR>  ||||reg||10
Z||000004||mrfixit58||05-06-2000||11:02 PM||||Thanks guys. I'll give it a try.||||reg||3
Z||000005||Rodney||05-07-2000||07:26 AM||||The Getrags all have steel end caps on the small end. The rest of the Fiero transmissions all have aluminum end caps.<P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
